# Material Recipes and Fabrication Process

The fabrication process involved developing two bio-based material composites using locally available waste materials: eggshells and spent coffee grounds (SCGs). The recipes were adapted from [Materiom](https://www.materiom.org/), an open-access database supporting bio-based material innovation.

The process combined digital fabrication, material experimentation and locally sourced ingredients to produce modular, bioreceptive prototypes.

## 1. Design and Preparation

The first stage involved identifying the materials, planning their procurement and preparing the digital design files.

A branching growth simulation pattern was selected for the installation. The pattern was generated using a Grasshopper script, with the simulation constrained within the desired boundary dimensions. The resulting geometry was transferred to Rhino, where a base and walls were added to create the mould geometry.

Initial material recipe tests, mould trials and shrinkage tests were conducted to determine the dimensional adjustments required for fabrication. These tests indicated an approximate shrinkage of 7–10% across the length, breadth and depth of the modules. The digital geometry was consequently scaled to compensate for this reduction.

The individual components of the modular grid were exported as STL files and processed using Creality Print to generate the G-code files for 3D printing.

## 2. Equipment and Mould Fabrication

Most of the equipment required for material preparation was sourced locally in Bir, either from the home kitchen or through borrowing from friends.

A Creality Ender-3 V3 SE 3D printer was purchased, along with PLA and PETG filaments, to facilitate the production and testing of moulds.

The relatively small scale of the mould components allowed the complete set to be printed within approximately two days.

**Mould fabrication considerations:**
- Export individual mould components as STL files.
- Import the files into Creality Print and check their orientation and dimensions.
- Generate G-code using the appropriate print settings for the selected filament.
- Add an external brim of approximately 3 mm where necessary to improve bed adhesion, particularly for components with limited contact area.
- Check the printed moulds for dimensional accuracy, surface irregularities and ease of material release.

## 3. Material Collection and Preparation

The eggshells and spent coffee grounds were collected from a local café in Bir. Both materials were cleaned and prepared before being incorporated into the composite mixtures.

